  {
   "post_number": 5,
   "username": "sem",
   "name": null,
   "created_at": "2020-12-18T23:25:00Z",
   "updated_at": "2020-12-18T23:25:00Z",
   "reply_to_post_number": null,
   "like_count": 1,
   "cooked": "<p>Minting TEC tokens would be an equivalent attack: somebody casts a vote that mints TEC tokens to who votes yes in case the vote passes. We are talking about the same, a voting 51% attack.</p>",
   "action": null,
   "source": {
    "source": "commoncrawl",
    "timestamp": "20230530084419",
    "url": "https://forum.tecommons.org/t/dandelion-voting-app-and-its-capabilities-mostly-extreme-use-cases/170"
   }
  },
  {
   "post_number": 6,
   "username": "sem",
   "name": null,
   "created_at": "2020-12-18T23:29:54Z",
   "updated_at": "2020-12-18T23:29:54Z",
   "reply_to_post_number": null,
   "like_count": 1,
   "cooked": "<blockquote>\n<p>Is it truley possible to use brightID (celeste) whilst staying anonymous? Through out the full pipeline, even at the brightID initial signup stage <img alt=\":thinking:\" class=\"emoji\" src=\"https://forum.tecommons.org/images/emoji/win10/thinking.png?v=9\" title=\":thinking:\"/> ?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think it’s an interesting question. I’m almost sure they don’t store names or profile photos, and they are only shared with who you are doing connections. On the other hand I dont’ know how much info the blockchain or the brightID graph reveals of you or your social network.</p>",
